1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Nc3 O-O 7. d4 exd4 8. Nd5 ({White falls into the well-known trap. If} 8. Nxd4 {a piece is lost.}) 8... b5 9. Bb3 Nxe4 ({Nxe4 is not good, as White gets too free a game.} 9... Bc5 {is far better.}) 10. Re1 Nf6 11. Nxd4 Nxd5 12. Bxd5 Bb7 13. Nf5 Bf6 14. Qg4 g6 ({Black's position is very difficult to defend. Perhaps} 14... Kh8 {is better than the text move.}) 15. Bf4 ({White should play} 15. Bh6 {first, forcing} Re8 {and then} 16. Bf4 {threatening both Rxe8+ and Nh6+.}) 15... d6 16. Rad1 Qc8 17. Nh6+ Kg7 18. Qg3 Ne7 19. Bxb7 Qxb7 20. Ng4 (20. Rxe7 {should be played}) 20... Nf5 21. Qh3 Bd8 22. Bh6+ ({Now} 22. Ne3 {should win; for if} Nxe3 23. Bh6+ Kh8 24. Qxe3 (24. Rxe3 $1 {Fritz}) 24... Rg8 25. Qc3+ f6 26. Re6 {etc. White gets no other chance of winning.}) 22... Nxh6 23. Qxh6+ Kg8 24. Rd3 f5 25. Re6 fxg4 $1 26. Rxg6+ hxg6 27. Qxg6+ {White can do no more than draw; Black has done the best he could in a very difficult position.} Kh8 {Sources: London Evening Standard, 29 March 1904; The Field, 2 April 1904; BCM, May 1904, p205; Westminster Gazette, 29 March 1904; Belfast News-Letter, 7 April 1904} (27... Kh8 {The Westminster Gazette & London Evening Standard give a further two half-moves:} 28. Qh6+ Kg8) 1/2-1/2

