1. c4 c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. g3 g6 4. Bg2 Bg7 5. e3 d6 6. Nge2 Nf6 7. d4 cxd4 8. exd4 Bf5 9. O-O O-O 10. a3 Qd7 11. b4 {White gets the better game from the opening, and Black has very little to do} Rfc8 12. f3 {[This move] has not much point; a steady advance on the queenside would be hard to meet.} h5 13. Be3 d5 {A timely move, though White gets three pawns to two.} 14. c5 Re8 15. b5 Na5 16. Bf4 Nc4 17. a4 Rad8 18. Nc1 Nh7 19. Re1 g5 20. Be5 $2 Bxe5 (20... f6 {would be answered by} 21. g4) 21. dxe5 e6 22. Qd4 Qc7 23. Bf1 {White cannot maintain his c-pawn, so wins the d-pawn instead.} Rc8 24. Bxc4 dxc4 25. Qxc4 Qxc5+ 26. Qxc5 {After the exchange of queens, there is no more interest in the game.} Rxc5 27. N1a2 Nf8 28. Ne4 Bxe4 29. Rxe4 Ng6 30. Rae1 Rd8 31. Nc1 Rc3 32. Kf2 Rc2+ 33. Ne2 Rd5 34. f4 gxf4 35. gxf4 Rd3 36. Rc1 Ra2 37. Rc7 Nh4 38. Re3 Rd4 39. Rg3+ Kh8 40. Rxf7 Nf5 41. Rf8+ {Sources: The Field, 2 April 1904; BCM, May 1904, p205} Kh7 {Source: London Evening Standard, 29 March 1904} 1/2-1/2

