1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Nf3 e6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Bc5 $2 6. Ndb5 d6 7. Be2 $2 (7. Be3 {is much stronger.}) 7... a6 8. Na3 Nf6 9. Bf3 O-O ({Black should play} 9... Bxa3) 10. Nc4 h6 11. O-O e5 $2 {Bad, as it weakens the d-pawn and gives two squares for the white knights.} 12. Ne3 b5 13. Ne2 Ne7 14. Ng3 Ng6 15. Nef5 Nh7 16. Qd3 Bxf5 $2 17. Nxf5 $2 (17. exf5 {wins a pawn, for there follows} Nf4 18. Bxf4 exf4 19. Ne2 $1 (19. Bxa8 {is bad, because of} fxg3 20. Bb7 Bxf2+ 21. Kh1 Qh4 22. h3 Ng5 {etc})) 17... Qf6 18. c3 Ne7 19. b4 (19. Ne3 {would maintain White's advantage; now Black gets the better game, but cannot quite win.}) 19... Nxf5 20. bxc5 Nh4 21. cxd6 Nxf3+ 22. gxf3 Rad8 23. Rd1 Rfe8 24. a4 Re6 25. Qe2 Ng5 26. Bxg5 Qxg5+ 27. Kh1 Rdxd6 28. axb5 Rxd1+ 29. Rxd1 Rg6 30. Qf1 axb5 31. Rd5 Qf4 {[The final move according to The Field & London Evening Standard]} 32. Rd3 {Sources: London Evening Standard, 29 March 1904; The Field, 2 April 1904; BCM, May 1904, p205} 1/2-1/2

