1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Nf3 g6 4. Bc4 {This is not a good square for the bishop; and curiously, throughout the game the piece is ill placed.} Bg7 5. O-O d6 6. h3 Nf6 7. Re1 Nd7 8. d3 Nde5 9. Bb3 e6 10. Bf4 Qf6 11. Bxe5 Nxe5 12. Nb5 (12. Nxe5 {is better; after this Black's bishops and better pawn position give him a decided advantage.}) 12... Nxf3+ 13. Qxf3 Qxf3 14. gxf3 Ke7 $1 15. c3 Bd7 16. Na3 b5 17. Nc2 a5 18. a3 e5 $1 19. Kh2 Bh6 20. Rad1 Bf4+ 21. Kg2 Raf8 22. d4 c4 23. Ba2 a4 $1 24. Nb4 Ke6 25. dxe5 Bxe5 26. Nd5 g5 27. Kh1 f5 28. exf5+ Rxf5 29. Kg2 Rhf8 30. Nc7+ Ke7 31. Nd5+ Kd8 32. Nb6 Bc6 33. Nd5 Rxf3 34. Re2 Bd7 {(adjudicated) Source: BCM, May 1904, p205} (34... Bd7 {at this stage the game was dismissed as a draw, as the match was already decided. Black has played extremely well, and an early victory is in sight. The simplest method is} 35. Rh1 Bc6 36. Rd1 R8f5 37. Bb1 Rd3 38. Bxd3 cxd3 39. Rxd3 Bf4 {wins easily}) 35. Rh1 {[This move not given in BCM, but according to The Field & the London Evening Standard is the last move played] Sources: The Field, 2 April 1904; London Evening Standard, 29 March 1904} 1/2-1/2

