1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 f6 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. cxd4 fxe5 8. Nxe5 Nf6 9. Be3 Bd6 10. f4 a6 11. O-O O-O 12. Nd2 (12. Qe1 {would be a good move, as Black's reply to the text move is rather difficult to answer.}) 12... Qb6 {Black threatens Nxe5 followed by Bxe5, so White is forced to exchange his well-placed knight.} 13. Nxc6 bxc6 ({Black should play} 13... Qxc6 {as the c-pawn is very weak and restrains the c8 bishop.}) 14. Nb3 Qc7 15. h3 g6 16. g4 {White with good judgment advances on the kingside.} Ne8 17. Qd2 Ng7 18. Rf2 Rf7 19. Raf1 Bd7 20. Nc5 Bxc5 21. dxc5 Qd8 22. Rf3 Qf8 23. Bd4 {The bishop enters very powerfully; Black can do nothing useful.} Bc8 24. b4 Ne8 25. Be5 Raa7 26. h4 a5 27. a3 axb4 28. axb4 Bd7 (28... Ba6 {is rather better}) 29. h5 gxh5 30. gxh5 Qh6 31. Rg3+ Ng7 32. Qb2 Be8 33. Ra1 Rxa1+ 34. Qxa1 Bd7 35. Bd6 $1 {Excellent; there is no defence to this. White has played a straightforward and good game.} Kh8 36. Qa8+ Ne8 37. Be5+ Rf6 38. Qb7 Qxh5 39. Qxd7 {Sources: London Evening Standard, 29 March 1904; The Field, 2 April 1904; BCM, May 1904, p207} 1-0

