1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 Bc5 5. Be3 Bb6 ({Unnecessary.} 5... Qf6 {is the usual move.}) 6. c3 Qf6 7. Bc4 Qe5 (7... Nge7 {is better. Black does not seem familiar with the opening.}) 8. O-O Nf6 9. f4 ({Black has lost time, and White could get the better of it with} 9. Nd2 {; strangely enough the e-pawn can now be taken safely.}) 9... Qxe4 10. Re1 O-O 11. Nd2 Qe8 12. Nf1 (12. N2f3 {is surely better.}) 12... Qd8 13. Nf5 d5 $1 {Good; the advance of this pawn frees Black's game.} 14. Bd3 d4 $1 15. cxd4 Bxf5 16. Bxf5 Nxd4 17. Bd3 Ne6 18. f5 (18. Bxb6 {would be better; White has no chance after the text.}) 18... Nc5 19. Bxc5 Bxc5+ 20. Kh1 Qd4 21. Nd2 $2 {A blunder, which costs a piece, but anyhow the game is hopeless.} Qxd3 22. Ne4 Qxd1 23. Nxf6+ gxf6 24. Raxd1 Rfe8 {("and after a few more moves White resigned" Source: London Evening Standard, 29 March 1904)} 25. Rxe8+ Rxe8 {"and White resigns after a few more moves." Sources: BCM, May 1904, p207; The Field, 2 April 1904} 0-1

