1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 Be7 5. Nxd4 ({White should play} 5. O-O {; the pawn can be recovered at any time.}) 5... d6 6. O-O Nf6 7. Nxc6 ({White would get a fine game by playing} 7. Nc3 {; as it is Black soon equalises.}) 7... bxc6 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Nc3 Bb7 10. Qf3 Qd7 11. Re1 Rfe8 12. Bf4 {The bishop would stand better at b2.} Rab8 13. b3 Ng4 14. Qh3 Ne5 15. Qg3 Bf6 16. Bg5 $2 {White overlooks the loss of the exchange; but he is lucky to find a way of regaining it; the position is most curious.} Nf3+ 17. Qxf3 Bxc3 18. e5 Bxa1 (18... Bxe5 {wins a pawn.}) 19. Bf5 Re6 20. Rxa1 c5 (20... dxe5 {might be played, but the pawn could not be retained.}) 21. Qh3 h6 22. Rd1 $1 Rbe8 23. Bxe6 {White must take the rook as Rxe5 is threatened.} Rxe6 24. Be3 Qc6 25. exd6 cxd6 26. c4 Rg6 27. f3 Bc8 (27... Qxf3 {would only draw.}) 28. g4 Bb7 {Source: BCM, May 1904, p207} 29. Rf1 {[This final move given by Hoffer in The Field, 2 April 1904 & London Evening Standard, 29 March 1904]} 1/2-1/2

